NAME
       groupadd - create a new group

SYNOPSIS
       groupadd [-g GID] [-r] [-f] [-o] [-K KEY=VALUE] GROUP

DESCRIPTION
       groupadd adds a group to /etc/group and /etc/gshadow. Without -g it
       takes the next free group ID at or above GID_MIN (1000) from
       /etc/login.defs; with -r it takes a system group ID below that.

       Groups let several users share files: make a group, add the users to
       it (usermod -aG or gpasswd -a), give a directory to the group
       (chgrp), and set the set-group-ID bit on it (chmod g+s) so new files
       there belong to the group too.

       Each user also has a private group of their own, made by useradd
       with the same name (user private groups).

OPTIONS
       -g GID, --gid GID
              Use this group ID. It must be free unless -o is given.

       -r, --system
              Create a system group (an ID below 1000), for services.

       -f, --force
              Succeed even if the group exists already (and pick another ID
              when the -g one is taken).

       -o, --non-unique
              Allow a GID that another group already has.

       -K KEY=VALUE
              Override a setting of /etc/login.defs, such as GID_MIN=5000.

EXAMPLES
       Create a group with a set ID:

           groupadd -g 3000 devops

       Set up a shared team directory:

           groupadd devops
           usermod -aG devops student
           mkdir /srv/devops
           chgrp devops /srv/devops
           chmod 2770 /srv/devops

       Check the result:

           getent group devops

FILES
       /etc/group, /etc/gshadow, /etc/login.defs

EXIT STATUS
       0 success, 4 the GID is in use, 9 the group name is in use.

SEE ALSO
       groupmod(8), groupdel(8), gpasswd(1), useradd(8), group(5)
